      "filename": "Kaufmann Stadium and Arrowhead Stadium, Truman Sports Complex, Kansas City, Missouri (14497073792).jpg",
      "title": "File:Kaufmann Stadium and Arrowhead Stadium, Truman Sports Complex, Kansas City, Missouri (14497073792).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Kaufmann_Stadium_and_Arrowhead_Stadium%2C_Truman_Sports_Complex%2C_Kansas_City%2C_Missouri_%2814497073792%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaufmann_Stadium_and_Arrowhead_Stadium,_Truman_Sports_Complex,_Kansas_City,_Missouri_(14497073792).jpg",
      "width": 4000,
      "height": 3000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ken Lund from Reno, Nevada, USA",
      "description": "The Ewing M. Kauffman Stadium, nicknamed \"The K\" and formerly known as Royals Stadium, is a Major League Baseball stadium located in Kansas City, Missouri, and home to the Kansas City Royals of the American League. Together with Arrowhead Stadium, home of the National Football League's Kansas City Chiefs, it is a part of the Truman Sports Complex. Since July 2, 1993, the venue had been known as Kauffman Stadium in honor of the Royals' founding owner, Ewing Kauffman.\n \nKauffman Stadium was built specifically for baseball during an era where building multisport \"cookie-cutter\" stadiums were commonplace. It is often held up along with Dodger Stadium in Los Angeles as one of the best examples of modernist stadium design.\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaufmann_Stadium\n\nArrowhead Stadium (commonly or simply Arrowhead) is a stadium located in Kansas City, Missouri, and home to the NFL's Kansas City Chiefs. It is part of the city's Truman Sports Complex (together with Kauffman Stadium). The stadium is commonly referred to as the \"Home of the CHIEFS\" at the beginning of every home game during the singing of the national anthem. It is the 27th largest stadium in North America and fifth largest NFL Stadium in seating capacity; behind AT&amp;T Stadium, Lambeau Field, MetLife Stadium, and FedExField. It is the largest sports facility, by capacity, in the state of Missouri. A $375 million renovation of the stadium was completed in 2010.\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrowhead_Stadium\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Text_of_Creative_Commons_...",
